Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your bench press is surging forward — keep this momentum and you'll hit 100 kg.",
  "summary": "This was a strong week for your bench press goal. Your chest strength index jumped from 106 to 112, the biggest single-period gain across all muscle groups, and your triceps also moved forward from 105 to 108 — both of those are the key drivers of a big bench press. You moved over 10,500 kg of total tonnage this week, which is solid output for your bodyweight of 72 kg. The main area to watch is your back, which dipped from 101 to 96, and your overall volume balance is heavily chest-focused at 41% of all sets. That's understandable given your goal, but keeping the supporting muscles strong will protect your shoulders and help you press more long-term. Overall, this was a productive week — you're clearly doing the work.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "Chest is the headline story this period, with the strength index climbing from 106 to 112 — a meaningful 5.7% gain and the strongest upward move in the dataset. Triceps also progressed from 105 to 108, which is encouraging since triceps are critical for locking out heavy bench press attempts. Shoulders held flat at 104, and biceps and legs both held at 100 and 102 respectively, indicating maintenance rather than growth. The one concern is the back, which regressed from 101 to 96. This is worth monitoring closely next period. Overall, the primary movers for your bench press goal are trending in the right direction."
  },
